#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106110 :question/problem "Suppose $\\mid c|= 14$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $θ = 60^{∘}$. Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d$." :question/worked-solution "Use the scalar-projection formula:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{comp}_{d}c=|c|\\cos\\theta=14\\cos60^\\circ=14\\left(\\frac12\\right)=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106120 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,3,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,1,-2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(3\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot (-2)-4\\cdot 2)\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-8\\mathbf{i}+10\\mathbf{j}-11\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-8,10,-11⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the corresponding parallelogram is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{b}\\hspace{0.167em}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{10}^{2}+(-11)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{285}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of the triangle is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{285}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106121 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-2,-3,-1⟩$ and $b = ⟨1, 2, 1⟩$." :question/worked-solution "The area of the parallelogram is $|a\\times b|$. Compute\n\n$$\na\\times b=\n\\begin{vmatrix}i&j&k\\\\-2&-3&-1\\\\1&2&1\\end{vmatrix}\n=-i+j-k.\n$$\n\nHence the area is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{(-1)^2+1^2+(-1)^2}=\\sqrt3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106122 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△ABC$, if $A(1, 1, 0)$, $B(3, 2, 2)$, $C(1, 2, 1)$." :question/worked-solution "Take two sides with common initial point $A$:\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\langle2,1,2\\rangle,\n\\qquad\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\langle0,1,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTheir cross product is $\\langle-1,-2,2\\rangle$, with magnitude $3$. A triangle has half the area of the parallelogram, so\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{Area}(\\triangle ABC)=\\frac12(3)=\\frac32.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106123 :question/problem "Calculate the area of the $△\\,PQR$, if $P(4,7,3)$, $Q(5,6,2)$, $R(3,8,3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\\mathbf{q}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(5\\mathbf{i}+6\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}-\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\mathbf{r}-\\mathbf{p} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+8\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(4\\mathbf{i}+7\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula to compute the cross product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 0-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(1\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\times \\overset{\\to }{PR}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,PQR$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106124 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $CH$ of the $△\\,ABC$, if $A(2,1,2)$, $B(1,2,1)$, $C(3,2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the length of the altitude $CH$ can be related to the area of $△\\,ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB\n$$\n\nSo, to find $CH,$ we first find ${A}_{△}.$ To start, we find the vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =-\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}-\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\mathbf{c}-\\mathbf{a} \\\\  & =(3\\mathbf{i}+2\\mathbf{j}+3\\mathbf{k})-(2\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{j}+((-1)\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 1)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =2\\mathbf{i}-2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the parallelogram spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AC}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe area of the triangle is half of the area of the respective parallelogram. So, the area of $△\\,ABC$ is\n\n$$\n{A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}A=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{2}=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nLet's also compute $AB$ since it appears in the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =|\\overset{\\to }{AB}| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, using the formula ${A}_{△}=\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{△} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot AB \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{2} & =CH\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ CH & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106125 :question/problem "Calculate the length of the altitude $RH$ of the $△PRQ$, if $P(1,-2, 3)$, $Q(3, 2, 3)$, $R(2, 1, 4)$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Use $PQ$ as the base. The side vectors are\n\n$$\n\\overrightarrow{PQ}=\\langle2,4,0\\rangle,\n\\qquad\\overrightarrow{PR}=\\langle1,3,1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe altitude from $R$ to the line $PQ$ is\n\n$$\nRH=\\frac{|\\overrightarrow{PQ}\\times\\overrightarrow{PR}|}{|\\overrightarrow{PQ}|}\n=\\frac{|\\langle4,-2,2\\rangle|}{2\\sqrt5}\n=\\frac{2\\sqrt6}{2\\sqrt5}=\\frac{\\sqrt{30}}5.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106126 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{a}=⟨3,1,-3⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨2,6,-2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨0,1,-4⟩$. Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$." :question/worked-solution "The source asks for $\\mathbf a\\cdot(\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c)$. Compute\n$$\\mathbf b\\times\\mathbf c=\\langle-22,8,2\\rangle,$$\nthen $\\langle3,1,-3\\rangle\\cdot\\langle-22,8,2\\rangle=-66+8-6=-64$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106127 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨2,-1,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{q}=⟨3,-3,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{r}=⟨1,-4,1⟩.$ Find $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p}).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p})$ is just the determinant of the matrix whose rows are given by $\\mathbf{r},$ $\\mathbf{q},$ and $\\mathbf{p}.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{r}\\cdot (\\mathbf{q}\\times \\mathbf{p}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}-(-4)\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}+1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 0-(-1)\\cdot 2)+4(3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 2)+(3\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot (-3)) \\\\  & =2-16+3 \\\\  & =-11.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106128 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $-\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (3\\mathbf{b}\\times (-2\\mathbf{c}))=24.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor out the constants and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (3\\mathbf{b}\\times (-2\\mathbf{c})) & =24 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot [\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})] & =24 \\\\ \\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{a}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =-4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand again we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\cdot (\\mathbf{c}\\times \\mathbf{a}) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106129 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a})$, if $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨2,3,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,-2,2⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the triple scalar product \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3,0⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2-6+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we swap $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{c}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{a}) &=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106130 :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}=⟨-2,1,8⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting given vectors into the formula for the scalar triple product, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨1,0,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,1,8⟩ \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+0\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106131 :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}=⟨0,-2,3⟩,$ $\\mathbf{b}=⟨4,-2,1⟩,$ and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨3,-1,2⟩,$ find $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c})$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =((-2)\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-1))\\mathbf{i}-(4\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{j}+(4\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 3)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-3\\mathbf{i}-5\\mathbf{j}+2\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =⟨-3,-5,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{a}\\cdot (\\mathbf{b}\\times \\mathbf{c}) & =⟨0,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,-5,2⟩ \\\\  & =0\\cdot (-3)+(-2)\\cdot (-5)+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106135 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$, $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$, given the points $A(1,2,-1)$, $B(2,3,1)$, $C(4,1,2)$, and $D(1,4,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ that span the parallelepiped:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =(2i+3j+k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =i+j+2k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,2⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =(4i+j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =3i-j+3k \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AD} & =d-a \\\\  & =(i+4j+2k)-(i+2j-k) \\\\  & =2j+3k \\\\  & =⟨0,2,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the volume of the parallelepiped, we need to take the absolute value of the triple product of the above vectors.\n\nComputing the triple product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot (\\overset{\\to }{AC}\\times \\overset{\\to }{AD}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-1\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+2\\cdot \\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 3)-(3\\cdot 3-0\\cdot 3)+2(3\\cdot 2-0\\cdot (-1)) \\\\  & =-9-9+12 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the corresponding parallelpiped spanned by $\\overset{\\to }{AB},$ $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AD}$ is\n\n$$\nV=|-6|=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106213 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=⟨-1,1,2⟩$ and $\\mathbf{q}=⟨4,x,1⟩.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 4+1\\cdot x+2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ x-2 & =0 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106214 :question/problem "Let $\\mathbf{p}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{q}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{p}$ onto $\\mathbf{q}$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{p}$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{q}$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{p}⊥\\mathbf{q}\\hspace{2em}⟹\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q}=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+1\\cdot 4+4\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{p}\\cdot \\mathbf{q} & =0 \\\\ 6x-12 & =0 \\\\ 6x & =12 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106309 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{x}=⟨3,5,0⟩$, $\\mathbf{y}=⟨3,2,0⟩$ and $\\mathbf{z}=⟨3,4,5⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3(2\\cdot 5-4\\cdot 0)-5(3\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 0)+0 \\\\  & =30-75 \\\\  & =-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is\n\n$$\nV=|-45|=45.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106310 :question/problem "Calculate the volume of the parallelepiped spanned by $\\mathbf{a}=⟨-3,0,-2⟩$, $\\mathbf{b}=⟨1,5,-3⟩$, and $\\mathbf{c}=⟨2,-1,4⟩$." :question/worked-solution "The volume is the absolute value of the scalar triple product. Here $b\\times c=\\langle17,-10,-11\\rangle$, so $$V=|a\\cdot(b\\times c)|=|-3(17)-2(-11)|=|-29|=29.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106346 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cce0e775722bbaaa8eeeb60a7cd097e8ad59b7c83c838f072416112aed657e33.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{21}{4}{\\text{ cm}}^{2}.$ Find $AC.$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the area of the triangle is \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}bc\\operatorname{sin}⁡A,\n$$\n\nwhere $∠A$ is between the sides of lengths $b$ and $c.$ \n\nHere\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =AB=3\\text{ cm}, \\\\ b & =AC, \\\\ m∠A & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nwe can calculate $b=AC,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot c\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡A \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 3\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot b\\cdot 3\\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{21}{4} & =\\frac{3b}{4} \\\\ 3b & =21 \\\\ b & =7\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that $AC=7\\text{cm}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106347 :question/problem "![](images/19/192cd7673f9186480bd6ab9955e56eab78b8a4e3bbec9d8d824922b5fd9c0527.png)\n\nThe area of ​​the triangle $△ABC$ is $\\frac{15\\sqrt{3}}{2}cm^{2}$. Determine $m∠A$." :question/worked-solution "The area formula gives $$\\tfrac12(5)(6)\\sin A=\\tfrac{15\\sqrt3}{2}\\implies\\sin A=\\tfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}.$$ The marked angle is acute in the given diagram, selecting $A=60^\\circ$ rather than its obtuse supplement."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106439 :question/problem "![](images/cc/cc23cf5b5df6c51aac404c9e6a95ba3cfbdb2279bcd3dea11c6d56966d91a8dd.png)\n\nRounded to the nearest tenth, what is the area of the quadrilateral $ABCD$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Split the convex quadrilateral along $BD$ into triangles with included angles $80^\\circ$ and $50^\\circ$. Add their areas: $$[ABCD]=\\tfrac12(5)(8)\\sin80^\\circ+\\tfrac12(10)(7)\\sin50^\\circ\\approx46.50771.$$ Rounded to the nearest tenth, the area is $46.5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106478 :question/problem "Suppose $|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|=18$ and the angle between vectors $\\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ is $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $\\mathbf{c}$ onto $\\mathbf{d}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{\\mathbf{d}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c} & =|\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{c}\\hspace{0.167em}|\\operatorname{cos}\\theta  \\\\  & =18\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-9\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114970 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t)$ with respect to $t,$ where $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are defined as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =(4{t}^{2}+9)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\left(\\frac{1}{t-2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =(4t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\operatorname{cos}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple rule and the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f(t) + 2\\mathbf g(t))' = \\mathbf f'(t) + 2\\mathbf g'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+9)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(-\\frac{1}{t-2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}2t)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+2{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(8t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+2(4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+3{e}^{3t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-2\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =8(t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(\\frac{1}{(t-2)^{2}}+6{e}^{3t})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}-6\\operatorname{sin}2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114971 :question/problem "Find $\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)'$ where $\\mathbf g (t) = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}\\ln   t \\\\ - t \\\\ {e}^{2 t}\\end{array} \\right]}$ and $u(t) = t.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\big( u(t) \\mathbf g(t) \\big)' = u'(t) \\mathbf{g}(t) + u(t) \\mathbf{g}'(t).\n$$\n\nLet's find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $u'(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{g}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+t\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{t} \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -t \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -t \\\\ 2t{e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+\\operatorname{ln}t \\\\ -2t \\\\ (2t+1){e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114972 :question/problem "Find $(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t),$ if $\\mathbf f(u) = \\ln u \\, \\mathbf i + (u^2+5) \\, \\mathbf j + 4u \\, \\mathbf k$ and $u(t) =2t^3.$" :question/worked-solution "By the chain rule, we have \n\n$$\n(\\mathbf f \\circ u)'(t) = \\mathbf{f}'(u(t)) u'(t).\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the derivative of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{ln}u)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}({u}^{2}+5)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(4u)\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}, \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})=6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{u(t)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2u(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}){u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2(2{t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+4\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot 6{t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+24{t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+24{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186114973 :question/problem "Find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t),$ given the following functions $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =\\sqrt{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{t}^{2}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\frac{1}{t}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f} \\cdot \\mathbf{g})(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t))\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+{e}^{t}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\cdot \\mathbf g)(t)\n&= \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{f}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t) + \\mathbf{f}(t) \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt}(\\mathbf{g}(t)) \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\,\\mathbf i +2t \\, \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac{1}{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + e^t \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) +\n\\left(\\sqrt{t} \\,  \\mathbf i + t^2 \\,  \\mathbf j \\right) \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{t^2}  \\, \\mathbf i + e^t \\, \\mathbf j  \\right) \\\\[3pt]\t\t \n&=\\dfrac{1}{2t\\sqrt{t}}  +2te^t    +  \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2}\\right)    + t^2e^t \\\\[3pt]\n&=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2}  -  \\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{t^2} +2te^t   + t^2e^t  \\\\[3pt]\n&= -\\dfrac{\\sqrt{t}}{2t^2} +\\left(2t+t^2\\right)e^t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115760 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 2t-8 \\\\ {5}^{t}\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)={t}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar function multiple rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'}={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find ${u}^{′}(t)$ and ${\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-8) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({5}^{t})\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 2 \\\\ {5}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})=2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u(t)\\mathbf{f}(t))^{'} & ={u}^{'}(t)\\mathbf{f}(t)+u(t){\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 2 \\\\ {5}^{t}\\operatorname{ln}5\\end{bmatrix}+2t\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 2t-8 \\\\ {5}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}t \\\\ 2{t}^{2} \\\\ {5}^{t}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2t\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 4{t}^{2}-16t \\\\ {5}^{t}\\cdot 2t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}t+2t\\operatorname{sin}t \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-16t \\\\ {5}^{t}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}5+{5}^{t}\\cdot 2t\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115762 :question/problem "Find $(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =20t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2+5t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & ={t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the dot product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\cdot \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\cdot \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\cdot {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(20\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+5\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot ({t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+({t}^{2}+t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})+(20t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+(2+5t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k})\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}+(2t+1)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}) \\\\  & =(20{t}^{3}-2t+5({t}^{2}+t))+(60{t}^{3}-2t+(2+5t)(2t+1)) \\\\  & =80{t}^{3}+15{t}^{2}+10t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115764 :question/problem "Find $(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)$ where\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3{t}^{2} \\\\ t+4 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}\\mathbf{g}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+2 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} \\\\ 5t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the cross product rule, we can write:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t)+\\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+4) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}6t \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2{t}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4t \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)\\times \\mathbf{g}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 6t & 1 & 0 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2{t}^{2} & 5t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}6t & 0 \\\\ t+2 & 5t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}6t & 1 \\\\ t+2 & -2{t}^{2}\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{f}(t)\\times {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 3{t}^{2} & t+4 & 8 \\\\ 1 & -4t & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}t+4 & 8 \\\\ -4t & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i}-\\begin{vmatrix}3{t}^{2} & 8 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{j}+\\begin{vmatrix}3{t}^{2} & t+4 \\\\ 1 & -4t\\end{vmatrix}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}\\times \\mathbf{g})^{'}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}5t \\\\ -30{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}37t+20 \\\\ 8-15{t}^{2} \\\\ -12{t}^{3}-t-4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}42t+20 \\\\ 8-45{t}^{2} \\\\ -24{t}^{3}-2t-6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115767 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{f}(u)=\\begin{bmatrix}2u \\\\ {u}^{2} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u(t)=2t+2$, then $(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(2u) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}\\hspace{0.167em}({u}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(3)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2u \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+2)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & =\\mathbf{f}'(u(t))u'(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2u(t) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2(2t+2) \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 8t+8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115768 :question/problem "Find $(u(t)\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ where $\\mathbf{g}(t)=⟨3{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}3-t,\\hspace{0.222em}3t⟩$ and $u(t) = t^{3}$." :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each component of the scalar product. First $u(t)g(t)=\\langle3t^5,3t^3-t^4,3t^4\\rangle$. Therefore $$(ug)'(t)=\\langle15t^4,9t^2-4t^3,12t^3\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115772 :question/problem "If $\\mathbf{f}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+(1-{u}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+12\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k}$ and $u(t)={t}^{3}$ then $(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the chain rule, we can write\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t)={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t)){u}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now find the derivatives of each function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(\\operatorname{cos}u)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(1-{u}^{2})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(12)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}+0\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{k} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2u\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\ {u}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})=3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}∘u)^{'}(t) & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(u(t))\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}(u(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2(u(t))\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot {u}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(-\\operatorname{sin}({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-2({t}^{3})\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}{t}^{3}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}-6{t}^{5}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115775 :question/problem "Differentiate $\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t)$ with respect to $t$, given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t) & =⟨3t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}6-t,\\hspace{0.222em}7t⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1-{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "By the sum rule, we have\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's find the derivatives of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6-t),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(7t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}7⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf{f}(t)+\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'} & ={\\mathbf{f}}^{'}(t)+{\\mathbf{g}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}7⟩+⟨2t,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+3,\\hspace{0.222em}-2t-1,\\hspace{0.222em}8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115776 :question/problem "Find the value of $(3\\mathbf{h}(t)-2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{'}$ at $t = 2$, if\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{h}(t) & =⟨2{t}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}t-3,\\hspace{0.222em}8+{t}^{3}⟩ \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t) & =⟨{t}^{2}+2t,\\hspace{0.222em}8,\\hspace{0.222em}2{t}^{3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Differentiate componentwise: $h'(t)=\\langle4t,1,3t^2\\rangle$ and $g'(t)=\\langle2t+2,0,6t^2\\rangle$. Thus $$(3h-2g)'(2)=3\\langle8,1,12\\rangle-2\\langle6,0,24\\rangle=\\langle12,3,-12\\rangle.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119559 :question/problem "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve  $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$  to $t = 2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/57/574a16e843a9c81e5fc9ddbbe2c0dc2c308ffcac4991bcbf48c7f45b4871c93b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119560 :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf g (t) = \\langle 3 - 2 \\sin 2t, \\: 1 - 2\\cos 2t \\rangle$ from $t=\\dfrac \\pi 3$ to $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/29/29c97e5963d451cc389944ef113764b2d15998448e773a02bb98c062207c2860.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡2t+16{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119675 :question/problem "![](images/85/8573aedcd26cba038b2db072560cd3d6ba7676542888a2d9b8a356713eaf6724.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t ,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom  $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119707 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u^2, \\: v^2, \\: u+v \\big\\rangle.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as $\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v.$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2u,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({v}^{2}),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}2v,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 2u & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2v & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2v,\\hspace{0.222em}-2u,\\hspace{0.222em}4uv⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119708 :question/problem "Find the surface area of the parametric surface defined by the equations \n\n$$\nx = u, \\quad \ny = v, \\quad \nz = 1 - u - v\n$$\n\nfor $-1 \\leq u \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 3.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u, \\: v, \\: 1 - u - v \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(1-u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, let's denote\n\n$$\nD = \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq u \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 3\\}.\n$$\n\nThen, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{3}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\text{ }\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119709 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\boxed{ {\\phantom{A A A A A A A A}}} \\: \\text{d}A, \n\\qquad D= \\{ (u,v) \\: : \\:  0 \\leq u \\leq 3, \\: 0 \\leq v \\leq 2 \\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface $\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle u, \\: v, \\: u^3-v \\big\\rangle$ for $0 \\leq u \\leq 3$ and $0 \\leq v \\leq 2.$ What is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf r (u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\,\\textrm d S = \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}0,\\hspace{0.222em}3{u}^{2}⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}({u}^{3}-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3{u}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\hspace{0.222em}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand its magnitude is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖ & =‖⟨-3{u}^{2},\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3{u}^{2})^{2}+(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}‖{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'}‖\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{\\color{black}\\sqrt{9{u}^{4}+2}}\\hspace{0.222em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
